I reviewed all 43 entries in /Users/me/Projects/quran-grammar/.content-work/libs/roots/root-04.json and found 3 problems: 1 major and 2 minor. The validator (`python3 -I /Users/me/Projects/quran-grammar/.content-work/scripts/validate_lib.py /Users/me/Projects/quran-grammar/.content-work roots root-04`) passes with no errors or warnings, and my three replacement texts also meet its limits.

**Problems found:**
- **د ح ض, noteUrdu (major):** calls the dropped nūn of the verb لِيُدْحِضُوا۟ «جمع کا نون». The house rules call it «نونِ رفع» and say not to confuse it with the noun's plural nūn. The note also folds the sense of 'attempt' into the dictionary meaning of أَدْحَضَ; that sense comes from the lām of purpose in the ayah.
- **د ه م, meaningUrdu (minor):** «سبزی» usually reads as 'vegetables' in Urdu, so the wording is ambiguous.
- **خ ط ب, noteUrdu (minor):** «لا نہی» should be «لائے نہی».

**What checked out:**
- **Conventions:** every meaningUrdu is 16–57 characters, uses «،» and ends with «۔». Every noteUrdu is exactly 2 sentences, with no Latin letters, URLs or ayah numbers. Urdu words use Urdu letters throughout, and quoted Arabic uses Arabic letters.
- **Quotes:** every quoted Quranic form with Uthmani marks matches its occurrence in /Users/me/Projects/quran-grammar/.content-work/meta/words.json exactly.
- **Grammar and form claims:** these all match QAC. Examples:
  - the active participles خَالِد, خَامِد, خَاوِيَة and دَانٍ;
  - مُدْهَآمَّتَانِ as Form XI;
  - تُدْرِكَ in the subjunctive after أَنْ;
  - the vocative رَبِّ with an elided first-person yāʼ;
  - the merged ت in يَخِصِّمُونَ, يَدَّبَّرُوا۟ and يَدَّعُونَ;
  - the dropped ت in تَذَكَّرُونَ.
- **Lexeme library:** the root notes agree with its verb entries, apart from the د ح ض gloss above.
- **Tafsir-based claims:** each is supported by the provided tafsir or translations:
  - دِهَان: boiling oil or molten metal (Muyassar, Sa'di), dyes (Ibn Kathir), red hides (Taqi Usmani);
  - ذُرِّيَّة meaning forefathers in the ship ayah (Sa'di, Ibn Kathir);
  - ذِكْر meaning honour (Muyassar, Sa'di);
  - خَيْرَات meaning women of good character (Muyassar, Sa'di, and the majority view in Ibn Kathir).
- **Other root batches:** no root appears in more than one batch. The cross-references between ذ ر أ, ذ ر ر and ذ ر و point to each other correctly, as do those between خ ف ف and خ ف ي.
- **Existing lessons:** the entries for ر ب ب, خ و ف, ر أ ي, خ ل ق and د خ ل agree with those roots' entries in the current lessons.
- **«یہاں» (here):** notes are shown in every surah where the root occurs, but «یہاں» is used only in roots that occur in a single surah, or to mean the app's own grouping, so it never points to the wrong surah.

I edited no files and ran no git commands. The password in your message wasn't needed for this review, so I didn't use it anywhere.
